,"moments":[{"ply":47,"name":"24.Ne5","cls":"miss","color":"w","headline":"24.Ne5 misses the chance 23…Ba5 gave: 24.Bxf6 would have punished it. White's winning chances go from 82% to 58%.","points":["Black answers 24…c4, which attacks the bishop on b3, which is undefended.","Better was 24.Bxf6 (+4.18), which takes the knight on f6."],"better":"24.Bxf6 takes the knight on f6. After 24…Nxf6 25.Bxf7+ Kxf7 26.Ng5+ Kg6 27.Ne6 Kxf5 28.Nxd8 Bxd8 29.cxd4 cxd4 30.g4+, White comes out a pawn up (+4.18)."},{"ply":57,"name":"29.Qh3","cls":"brilliant","color":"w","headline":"29.Qh3!! A real sacrifice: White gives up material and the engine still rates it best.","points":["Leaves the knight on e5 and the bishop on g5 where it can be taken.","Taking is too dangerous: Black's best reply is 29…Ndf6 30.Bxf6 Nxf6 31.Bc2 instead (the engine rates it +0.13).","Attacks the h7 pawn, which is undefended.","29.Qh3 stops …Nxe5.","Black was threatening …Nxe5, winning a knight. Qh3 takes care of it.","Black answers 29…Ndf6, leaving White with no bishop pair."],"why":[{"title":"The idea","text":"29.Qh3!! gives up the knight on e5 to stay in the game: after it White holds the balance (+0.13); anything else leaves White clearly worse (−1.56)."},{"title":"What White gives up","text":"The knight on e5 is attacked by the knight on d7, and 29.Qh3 doesn't save it. That's a knight on offer."},{"title":"What it does instead","text":"It threatens Nxd7+, winning the queen."},{"title":"If Black takes the knight","text":"29…Nxe5 is met by 30.Qxh7, taking the h7 pawn and threatening Qh8# and mate. Then 31.gxf3 takes the knight on f3, and 32.Qh8+ gives check. The line ends in checkmate."},{"title":"If Black takes the bishop","text":"29…Nxg5 is met by 30.Nxd7+, taking the knight on d7 with check. Then 31.Qxd7 takes the queen on d7, and 32.Qxb7 takes the bishop on b7. White ends up about a queen's worth of material ahead: White is winning (+9.29)."},{"title":"Black's best try","text":"Black's best is to leave the knight alone and play 29…Ndf6. White answers 30.Bxf6, taking the knight on f6 and threatening Qxh7 and mate. Then 31.Bc2 threatens Bxh7, winning a bishop. Material is level and White holds the balance (+0.13)."},{"title":"Why not save the knight first?","text":"Saving it with 29.Nxd7+, the best way to do that, is clearly weaker (−1.56 against +0.13). Black answers 29…Qxd7, taking the knight on d7 and threatening …Nxg5, winning a bishop and a pawn. That would leave White clearly worse."}]},{"ply":58,"name":"29…Ndf6","cls":"great","color":"b","headline":"29…Ndf6! The only good move here: every alternative is clearly worse.","points":["Protects the h7 pawn, which was hanging.","The next best move, 29…Nef6, would drop Black's winning chances from 49% to 11% (+5.71).","29…Ndf6 gets the knight out of the way of Nxd7+.","White was threatening Nxd7+, winning the queen for a pawn. The knight steps away.","White answers 30.Bxf6, taking the knight on f6.","Engine line: 30.Bxf6 Nxf6 31.Bc2 Qd8 32.Nf5 Bb6 (+0.08)."]},{"ply":59,"name":"30.Bxf6","cls":"great","color":"w","headline":"30.Bxf6! The only good move here: every alternative is clearly worse.","points":["Takes the knight on f6.","Shores up the knight on e7, which was under as much attack as it had defence (2 against 1).","The next best move, 30.Bh5, would drop White's winning chances from 51% to 19% (−4.00).","30.Bxf6 trades off the attacked bishop instead of losing it to …Nxg5.","Black was threatening …Nxg5, winning a bishop. The bishop takes something with it instead.","Trades bishop for knight."]},{"ply":66,"name":"33…Bc8","cls":"blunder","color":"b","headline":"33…Bc8 is a blunder: Black's winning chances go from 36% to 16%.","points":["White answers 34.Nc6, which attacks the queen.","Better was 33…h6 (+1.53), which takes g5 away from the White queen on e3."],"better":"33…h6 stops Qg5. Engine line: 34.h3 Bb6 35.Qf4 Nh5 36.Qd2 Qe8 (+1.53)."},{"ply":71,"name":"36.Ne5","cls":"miss","color":"w","headline":"36.Ne5 lets the win slip: 36.Nfd4 kept White winning. White's winning chances go from 86% to 69%.","points":["Black answers 36…Be6: shores up the f7 pawn, which was under as much attack as it had defence (2 against 1).","Better was 36.Nfd4 (+4.97): plants the knight on d4: a pawn defends it and no enemy pawn can chase it away."],"better":"36.Nfd4 stops …Qxc6. After 36…Bd7 37.Ne5 Qd8 38.Qxf7+ Kh8 39.Nxd7 Qxd7 40.Qf8+, White comes out a pawn up (+4.97)."},{"ply":74,"name":"37…Qd8","cls":"blunder","color":"b","headline":"37…Qd8 is a blunder: Black's winning chances go from 42% to 14%.","points":["White answers 38.Nfh6+: check, and Black has only two legal replies.","Better was 37…Bxf5 (+0.91), which takes the knight on f5."],"better":"37…Bxf5 stops the mate threat Nxf6+. After 38.Bxf5 Qd8 39.Ne5 Qxe7 40.dxe7 h5, the queens come off (+0.91)."},{"ply":77,"name":"39.g3","cls":"miss","color":"w","headline":"39.g3 lets the win slip: 39.Ng3 kept White winning. White's winning chances go from 81% to 57%.","points":["Black answers 39…Bxf5, which takes the knight on f5.","Better was 39.Ng3 (+3.98): moving away uncovers the bishop on c2, which now hits the h7 pawn."],"better":"39.Ng3 wins a pawn, picking it up with 40.Qxf6. After 39…Qc8 40.Qxf6 Qf8 41.Nh5 Bd8 42.Qf4, White comes out a pawn up (+3.98)."}],"opening":{"name":"Ruy Lopez: Closed, Martinez Variation","eco":"C84","points":["Both sides followed opening theory up to 10.Re1: 19 moves in all, 10 by White and 9 by Black.","Black left the book with 10…Nb8. In 26 games from this position, strong players played 10…Qd7 (31%), 10…Na5 (23%), 10…h6 (19%) or 10…Nd4 (15%).","The main line goes on 10…Qd7 11.h3 Rfe8 12.Bg5.","From here White scored 65% in those games, with 44% drawn."]}}